---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CURRENT, PRODUCTS, product, type ProductId } from '../lib/products';
import { LINES, FAMILY_SENTENCE } from '../lib/family';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const flowVersion = (await getCollection('releases')).filter((r) => r.data.product === 'flow' && r.data.published).map((r) => r.data.version).sort((a, b) => b.localeCompare(a, undefined, { numeric: true }))[0];

// Yes means the product has it (whatever its status on that product's page);
// the columns are IoT, Edge, Gateway, Edge Relay.
const rows: [string, boolean, boolean, boolean, boolean][] = [
  ['Every protocol driver, MQTT included', true, true, true, true],
  ['Entities, automations, helpers, scripts, logbook', true, true, true, false],
  ['Notifications and ISA-18.2 alarms', true, true, true, false],
  ['MCP for AI agents', true, true, true, false],
  ['MQTT discovery (Shelly, ESPHome, Tasmota, Zigbee2MQTT), read and forward only on the relay', true, true, true, true],
  ['Network discovery (mDNS, SSDP, DHCP)', true, true, true, false],
  ["Historian targets in the plant's own database", true, true, true, false],
  ['UniFi network driver, read-only (the screens and events need the UI)', true, true, true, true],
  ['Backups and restore', true, true, true, false],
  ['Dashboards, per role and per screen, with panel mode', true, true, true, false],
  ['Store-and-forward over Sparkplug B', false, true, true, true],
  ['OPC UA server', false, true, true, false],
  ['Fleet management of edge relays', false, false, true, false],
  ['Multi-site roll-ups', false, false, true, false],
  ['Scheduled reports', false, false, true, false],
  ['A web UI and a login', true, true, true, false],
];
const yes = (b: boolean) => (b ? 'Yes' : 'No');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop products: seven on four release lines"
  description="Seven products for the plant floor: IoT, Edge, Gateway, Edge Relay, Flow, Cascade, and HotLoop for CODESYS. What each has, and which release line it ships on."
  ogAlt="The HotLoop lineup: IoT, Edge, Gateway, Edge Relay, Flow, Cascade, and HotLoop for CODESYS."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <span class="label">Products</span>
      <h1>Seven products. Four release lines.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        {FAMILY_SENTENCE} The first four share one codebase and one version, and every one of them speaks every protocol,
        MQTT included, so what you're really picking is how much of the plant the box has to run.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap body">
    {LINES.map((l) => (
      <div class="line">
        <p class="line-head">{l.released ? <span class="chip ok">{l.label} line, {l.id === 'flow' ? flowVersion : l.version}</span> : <span class="chip warn">{l.label} line, not released yet</span>} <span class="muted">{l.blurb}</span></p>
        <div class="grid grid-2 cards">
          {l.members.map((m) => (
            <a class="card product reveal" href={m.href}>
              <h2>{m.name}</h2>
              <p class="tag">{m.tagline}</p>
              {l.id === 'core' && <p class="db"><span class="label">Database</span> {product(m.id as ProductId).databases}</p>}
            </a>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}

    <h2 class="sub">Side by side</h2>
    <p class="muted intro">
      What each product has, not whether it's finished. For that, open the product's page: every piece on it says
      whether it ships today, is unverified, or hasn't been built.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="data stack">
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>IoT</th><th>Edge</th><th>Gateway</th><th>Edge Relay</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map(([name, a, b, c, d]) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{name}</td>
              <td data-label="IoT" class={a ? 'y' : 'n'}>{yes(a)}</td>
              <td data-label="Edge" class={b ? 'y' : 'n'}>{yes(b)}</td>
              <td data-label="Gateway" class={c ? 'y' : 'n'}>{yes(c)}</td>
              <td data-label="Edge Relay" class={d ? 'y' : 'n'}>{yes(d)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
          <tr>
            <td>Database</td>
            {PRODUCTS.map((p) => <td data-label={p.name.replace('HotLoop ', '')}>{p.databases.split('.')[0]}</td>)}
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Install today</td>
            {PRODUCTS.map((p) => <td data-label={p.name.replace('HotLoop ', '')}>{p.installable ? 'Yes' : 'Not yet'}</td>)}
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-2 notes">
      <div class="stack">
        <h2 class="sub">Moving up keeps your data</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Outgrowing the Pi is supposed to cost you an image pull, not a migration project. Every product runs the
          same schema, so going from IoT to Edge to the Gateway is designed to be an image swap on the same database,
          with your devices, entities, rules, alarms, history, and users already there. Going back down deletes
          nothing: what the smaller product doesn't have just sits there until you come back. The Podman (Quadlet)
          move has been run, IoT to Edge to the Gateway on one database volume. The same move as a Helm (Kubernetes)
          upgrade on a real cluster has not been run, and neither has going back down a product, so don't try either on a box you care about.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <h2 class="sub">No keys, no tiers</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          No license keys, and nothing unlocks at runtime. The product is what got compiled, so a feature that isn't
          in your image can't be flipped on by accident, and nobody can flip one off to squeeze you later. All of
          HotLoop is under one license, the HotLoop Business Source License 1.1: free for home and lab, free for
          business through {' '}<a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNet</a>. <a href="/license/">What that means for you</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="muted flow">
      Where each one is headed: the <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/roadmap/">HotLoop roadmap</a> for the four core
      products, the <a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/flow/roadmap/">Flow roadmap</a> and the
      {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/cascade/roadmap/">Cascade roadmap</a>, and the
      {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/codesys/roadmap/">HotLoop for CODESYS roadmap</a>. The docs for all seven are at
      {' '}<a href="https://docs.hotloop.io/products/">docs.hotloop.io</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s6); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .body { padding-bottom: var(--hl-s8); }
  /* Four products: two by two, never three and an orphan. */
  .line { margin-bottom: var(--hl-s6); }
  .line-head { margin: 0 0 var(--hl-s3); display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s2) var(--hl-s3); align-items: center; }
  .cards { margin-bottom: var(--hl-s4); grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 420px), 1fr)); }
  a.product { display: grid; gap: var(--hl-s3); align-content: start; text-decoration: none; color: inherit; transition: transform var(--hl-transition-fast), border-color var(--hl-transition-fast); }
  a.product:hover { transform: translateY(-2px); border-color: var(--hl-orange); }
  .product .head { justify-content: space-between; }
  .product h2 { font-size: var(--hl-fs-xl); margin: 0; }
  .product .tag { color: var(--hl-ink); font-weight: 600; font-size: var(--hl-fs-base); }
  .product .db { padding-top: var(--hl-s3); border-top: 1px solid var(--hl-border); }
  .product .db .label { margin-right: var(--hl-s2); }
  .product .db .today { display: block; margin-top: var(--hl-s1); font-size: var(--hl-fs-xs); }
  h2.sub { font-size: var(--hl-fs-xl); }
  .intro { margin: var(--hl-s3) 0 var(--hl-s5); max-width: 70ch; }
  td.y { color: var(--hl-ok-text); font-weight: 600; }
  td.n { color: var(--hl-ink-soft); }
  .notes { margin-top: var(--hl-s8); gap: var(--hl-s7); }
  .flow { margin-top: var(--hl-s7); max-width: 70ch; }
</style>
